session_callback: close the dedicated connection on a subscribe timeout

create_new_cb() opens a brand new loopback connection (bb_gethandle())
for every BB_SET_EVENT_SUBSCRIBE call, then sends the subscribe request
and waits for the daemon's reply. Every other session type that does
this same send-and-wait dance -- session_debug.c, session_socket.c,
session_hotplug.c, session_platform_ctrl.c -- calls bs_trig_close() on
a failure/timeout to close that dedicated connection and let its reader
thread exit. This one didn't: it just returned -1, leaving the
connection, its reader thread, and (server-side) its cb_dev/cb_rpc
registration running forever.

Confirmed live: a caller retrying an event-subscribe with a bounded
timeout (needed so a transient daemon-side fault, e.g. an SDIO
transport hiccup mid-request, doesn't wedge it forever -- see the
sibling fix to bs_deinit()'s lost-wakeup race) leaked one connection +
thread + server-side registration per timed-out attempt. Under
sustained retries during an extended fault, these accumulated until a
later transport-fault cleanup had to wait on dozens of them and got
stuck on an unresponsive one -- the mechanism behind an apparent 100+
thread pileup and a permanently frozen daemon.

bs_trig_close() is exactly what every sibling call site already relies
on for this case; this makes create_new_cb() do the same.

diff --git a/app/ar8030/session_callback.c b/app/ar8030/session_callback.c
index 3010a9e..440e0f6 100644
--- a/app/ar8030/session_callback.c
+++ b/app/ar8030/session_callback.c
@@ -139,6 +139,20 @@ static int create_new_cb(bb_dev_handle_t* pdev, bb_set_event_callback_t* pcb, in
     int ret = bs_send_usbpack_and_wait(&cbsession->base, &pack, timeout);
     if (ret) {
         printf("callback init err , remote exit\n");
+        /* Every other session type (session_debug.c, session_socket.c,
+         * session_hotplug.c, session_platform_ctrl.c) calls bs_trig_close()
+         * here to close the dedicated connection bb_gethandle() opened for
+         * this call and let its reader thread exit -- this one didn't.
+         * Confirmed live: repeated timed-out subscribe attempts (e.g. a
+         * caller retrying with a bounded timeout after the daemon's SDIO
+         * transport faults) leaked one connection + reader thread + a
+         * matching server-side cb_dev/cb_rpc registration per attempt,
+         * until a later transport fault's cleanup had to wait on dozens of
+         * them and stuck forever on an unresponsive one -- the mechanism
+         * behind an apparent 100+ thread pileup and permanent daemon
+         * freeze. bs_trig_close() is what every sibling call site already
+         * relies on for this exact case. */
+        bs_trig_close(&cbsession->base);
         return -1;
     }
